    {
      "id": "ES-C346-escuela",
      "sequence": 6220,
      "title": "la escuela — the school, which began as spare time",
      "headword": "la escuela",
      "romanization": "la escuela",
      "gloss": "school — Latin schola from Greek skholē, spare time; English school, scholar and scholastic keep the leisure that turned into study",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:2e8c9f6a1786aad3",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say heat, then age. (El calor; la edad.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "estudiante — the person who goes there."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el maestro — the person who waits there."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "es-KUE-la, three beats with the weight on the second. Spanish will not begin a word with sc-, so it puts an e in front. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la escuela — \"the school.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Greek skholḗ did not mean study. It meant spare time — the hours a free Athenian was not obliged to spend on work."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did he do with them? He went and argued in public. So the word for the leisure slid over to the arguing, and then to the group that gathered to argue, and at last to the building they gathered in. Latin borrowed it as schola, and Spanish worked it down to escuela."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English kept the same word twice over: a school, a scholar, a scholarship, and anything scholastic. All four rest on a Greek word for time off."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "There is a small piece of Spanish machinery to notice too. Latin schola begins sc-, and Spanish mouths do not start a word that way, so an e grows in front. The same e is at the front of España and estudiante."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first of five places you can point at in a town."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la escuela",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la escuela*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una escuela",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una escuela*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "¿dónde está la escuela?",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *¿dónde está la escuela?*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did skholḗ mean in Greek? (Spare time, leisure.) And what English word for a learner comes off it? (Scholar.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C346-iglesia",
      "sequence": 6230,
      "title": "la iglesia — the church, named after the crowd",
      "headword": "la iglesia",
      "romanization": "la iglesia",
      "gloss": "church — Latin ecclesia from Greek ekklesia, the people called out to assemble; English ecclesiastical is the same gathering",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:01c99e3b74c8414d",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say age, then school. (La edad; la escuela.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la gente — who fills it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el pueblo — where it usually stands."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "i-GLE-sia, three beats with the weight on the second. The g is hard, as in gato, and -sia runs together into one beat. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la iglesia — \"the church.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Greek ekklēsía was a political word before it was a religious one. It is ek, out, plus kaleîn, to call: the citizens called out of their houses by a herald, gathered in the open to vote."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Early Christians borrowed that word for their own gatherings, Latin took it as ecclēsia, and Spanish wore it down to iglesia."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the name belongs to the people, not to the walls. A Spanish speaker saying la iglesia is naming a crowd that has been summoned, and only afterwards the roof over it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English kept the Greek shape in ecclesiastical, and the kaleîn half turns up again in council and in a calendar, whose first day was called out aloud each month."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two buildings a town has, and both are named for the people inside."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la iglesia",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la iglesia*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una iglesia",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una iglesia*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la iglesia del pueblo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la iglesia del pueblo*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What two Greek pieces make ekklēsía? (Ek, out, and kaleîn, to call.) And what did it name first — the crowd or the building? (The crowd.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C346-mercado",
      "sequence": 6240,
      "title": "el mercado — the market, and the trade behind mercy",
      "headword": "el mercado",
      "romanization": "el mercado",
      "gloss": "market — Latin mercatus from merx, goods; English market, merchant, commerce and even mercy grow out of the same trade",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:ef1f53638463b73d",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say school, then church. (La escuela; la iglesia.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la tienda — the smaller thing inside it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "comprar — what you go there to do."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mer-KA-do, three beats with the weight on the second. The r is one tap, and the d between vowels is soft. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el mercado — \"the market.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin merx, mercis meant goods, wares — the things themselves. Mercātus was the act of trading them, and then the place where the trading happened."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English took the same root by two roads. Through Latin came commerce, mercantile and mercenary, a soldier who fights for goods rather than for a flag. Through Old French came merchant and market."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The strange one is mercy. Latin mercēs was the pay owed for work or wares. In church Latin it drifted: a mercy became a payment made where none was owed at all, a wage handed to somebody who had not earned it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So a market square and an act of mercy come out of the same Latin heap of goods."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three places, and three different reasons a town gathers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el mercado",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el mercado*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "un mercado grande",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *un mercado grande*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "¿dónde está el mercado?",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *¿dónde está el mercado?*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did Latin merx mean? (Goods, wares.) And what did mercēs mean before it meant mercy? (The pay owed.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C346-oficina",
      "sequence": 6250,
      "title": "la oficina — the office, which used to be a workshop",
      "headword": "la oficina",
      "romanization": "la oficina",
      "gloss": "office — Latin officina, a workshop, squeezed out of opificina from opus work and facere to make; English office, officer and opus are the same making",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:b746f2d5d94f1349",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say church, then market. (La iglesia; el mercado.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la mesa — what stands in it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el lugar — the general word this narrows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "o-fi-SI-na, four beats with the weight on the third. The c before i is soft — an s across the Americas. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la oficina — \"the office.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin had opus, work, and facere, to make. Stick them together and you get opifex, a work-maker, a craftsman. The place he worked was an opificīna."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four syllables was too many for daily speech, so Latin itself squeezed the middle out and said officīna — a workshop, a forge, a place where something was actually being made."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish kept the workshop sense for centuries. The desk and the paperwork are a late arrival; the word underneath still means the room where the work is done."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English took office, officer, official and officious off the same stem, and kept opus itself for a composer's work. Operate, opera and operation are all opus as well."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four places, and one of them is a workshop wearing a suit."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la oficina",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la oficina*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una oficina nueva",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una oficina nueva*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la oficina está en la plaza",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la oficina está en la plaza*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What two Latin words hide inside officīna? (Opus, work, and facere, to make.) And what was an officīna before it was an office? (A workshop.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C346-estacion",
      "sequence": 6260,
      "title": "la estación — the station, a place for standing still",
      "headword": "la estación",
      "romanization": "la estación",
      "gloss": "station — Latin statio from stare, to stand; a station is a standing-place, and English station, stationary, statue and stable stand on the same verb",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:816d9b2b20b3b379",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say market, then office. (El mercado; la oficina.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la parada — the smaller stop this outgrew."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "estar — the verb hiding at the front of it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "es-ta-SIÓN, three beats with the weight on the last, which is why it carries a written accent. The c before i is soft. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la estación — \"the station.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin stāre meant to stand, and a statiō was a standing-place: a post where a sentry was set, a road-house where horses were changed, an anchorage where ships stood at rest."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Every later sense grows out of that standing. A railway estación is where a train stands. A radio estación is a fixed point on a dial. And a season of the year is an estación too, because the sun appears to stand at the same height for a stretch of weeks."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The same stāre is already in your mouth. Estar is that verb, worn down by two thousand years of use — which is why estar is the to be you use for where a thing stands rather than what it permanently is."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English is full of it as well: station, stationary, status, statue, stable, stance, estate and circumstance."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five places you can name and ask for: a school, a church, a market, an office and a station."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
